WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.770
Welcome, Russ. I'm really glad you could join us here today.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.770
Thank you. Thank you, Andrew.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.770
So, you know, today you're the director of research at Apple,

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.770
and you also have a faculty and professor at Carnegie Mellon University.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:19.770
So I'd love to hear a bit about your personal story.

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:23.770
How did you end up doing this deep learning work that you do?

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:26.770
Yeah, it's actually, to some extent,

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:30.770
it was I started in deep learning, to some extent, by luck.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:34.770
I did my master's degree at Toronto, and then I took a year off.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.770
I was actually working in the financial sector. It's a little bit surprising.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:42.770
And at that time, I wasn't quite sure whether I want to go for my PhD or not.

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.770
And then something happened, something surprising happened.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:49.770
I was going to work one morning, and I bumped into Jeff Hinton.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.770
And Jeff told me, hey, I have this terrific idea.

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.770
Come to my office. I'll show you.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.770
And so we basically walked together,

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:01.770
and he started telling me about, you know, these Boltzmann machines

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.770
and contrastive divergence and some of the tricks,

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:08.770
which I didn't at that time quite understand what he was talking about.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:14.770
But that really, really excited, that was very exciting, and really excited me.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:20.770
And then basically, within three months, I started my PhD with Jeff.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.770
So that was kind of like the beginning,

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:27.770
because that was back in 2005, 2006.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:31.770
And this is where some of the regional deep learning algorithms

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.770
using restricted Boltzmann machines and supervised petraining

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.770
were kind of popping up.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.770
And so that's how I started.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.770
It was really, you know, that one particular morning,

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:48.770
when I bumped into Jeff, completely changed my future career moving forward.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:52.770
And then, in fact, you were co-author on, you know,

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.770
one of the very early papers on restricted Boltzmann machines

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:59.770
that really helped with this research of neural networks and deep learning.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.770
Tell me a bit more of what that was like, you know, working on that seminar.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:07.770
Yeah, this was actually a really, this was an exciting year.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.770
It was my first year as a PhD student,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:15.770
and Jeff and I were trying to explore these ideas

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.770
of using restricted Boltzmann machines and using pre-training tricks

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.770
to train multiple layers.

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:24.770
And specifically, we were trying to focus on auto-encoders.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.770
You know, how do we do a non-linear extension of PCA effectively?

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.770
And it was very exciting, because we got these systems

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.770
to work on MNIST digits, which was exciting.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.770
But then the next steps for us were to really see

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:41.770
whether we can extend these models to dealing with faces.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:44.770
I remember we had this elevated faces dataset.

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.770
And then we started looking at, can we do compression for documents?

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.770
So we started looking at all these different data, you know,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.770
real value, count, binary.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:59.770
And throughout, you know, a year it was, I was a first year PhD student,

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.770
so it was a big learning experience for me.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:06.770
But really, within six or seven months,

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:10.770
we were able to get really interesting results and really good results,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:14.770
something that we, you know, we were able to train these very deep auto-encoders.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.770
This is something that you couldn't do at that time

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:21.770
using sort of traditional optimization techniques.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:27.770
And then it, you know, it turned out into a really, really exciting paper for us.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:33.770
That was super exciting year, because it was a lot of learning for me.

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.770
But at the same time, the results turned out to be, you know,

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:41.770
really, really impressive for what we were trying to do.

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:44.770
So in the early days of this resurgence of deep learning,

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:48.770
a lot of the activity was centered on restricted Boltzmann machines

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.770
and then deep Boltzmann machines.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.770
There's still a lot of exciting research there being done,

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:57.770
including some in your group, but what's happening with Boltzmann machines

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.770
and restricted Boltzmann machines?

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:01.770
Yeah, that's a very good question.

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:06.770
I think that in the early days, the way that we were using restricted Boltzmann machines

00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:10.770
is you sort of can imagine training a stack of these restricted Boltzmann machines

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.770
that would allow you to learn effectively one layer at a time.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:17.770
And there's a good theory behind, you know, when you add a particular layer,

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:20.770
it improves the variation of bound and so forth under certain conditions.

00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:24.770
So there was a theoretical justification, and these models were working quite well

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:28.770
in terms of being able to pre-train these systems.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:35.770
And then around 2009, 2010, once the computer started showing up, you know, GPUs,

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.770
then a lot of us started realizing that actually directly optimizing

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:45.770
these deep neural networks was, you know, was giving similar results,

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.770
or even better results.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:51.770
So just that the backprop was about the pre-training or the restricted Boltzmann machine?

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:52.770
That's right, that's right.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.770
And that's sort of over, you know, three or four years,

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.770
and it was exciting to the whole community because people thought that,

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:02.770
wow, you can actually train these deep models using these pre-training mechanisms.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.770
And then, you know, with more compute, people started realizing

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.770
that you can just basically do standard backprop navigation,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:12.770
something that we couldn't do back in 2005 or, you know, 2004,

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.770
because it would take us months to do it on CPUs.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:20.770
And so that was a big change.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.770
The other thing that I think that we haven't really figured out

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:27.770
what to do with, you know, Boltzmann machines and deep Boltzmann machines,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.770
I believe they're very powerful models because you can think of them as generative models.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:35.770
You know, they try to model complex distributions in the data.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.770
But when we start looking at learning algorithms, learning algorithms right now,

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.770
they require using, you know, Markov chain Monte Carlo

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:48.770
and variational learning and such, which is not a scalable as backpropagation algorithm.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:55.770
So we yet have to figure out more efficient ways of training these models

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.770
and also the use of convolution.

00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:02.770
It's something that's fairly difficult to integrate into these models.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:06.770
I remember some of your work on using probabilistic Mark's pulling

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:11.770
for sort of building these generative models of different objects

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:16.770
and using these ideas of convolution was also very exciting.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:19.770
But at the same time, it's still extremely hard to train these models.

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.770
How likely is work?

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:22.770
Yes, how likely is work, right?

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:28.770
So we still have to figure out what are on the other side,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.770
some of the recent work using variational encoders, for example,

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:34.770
which could be viewed as directed versions of Boltzmann machines.

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:39.770
We have figured out ways of training these models was a work by Max Welling

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:44.770
and Dirk Kingma on using, you know, reparameterization tricks.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:49.770
And now we can use backpropagation algorithm within the stochastic system,

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:52.770
which is driving a lot of progress right now.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:57.770
But we haven't quite figured out how to do that in the case of Boltzmann machines.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:02.770
So that's actually a very interesting perspective I actually wasn't aware of,

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:05.770
which was in an earlier era where computers were slower,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:10.770
that the RBM pre-training was really important.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:16.770
There was only faster computation that drove switching to standard backprop.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:20.770
You know, in terms of the evolution of communities thinking in deep learning,

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:24.770
another topic I know you spent a lot of time thinking about is the generative,

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.770
unsupervised versus supervised approaches.

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:31.770
Do you want to share a bit about how your thinking about that has evolved over time?

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:35.770
Yeah, I think that's a really, I feel like it's a very important topic,

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:42.770
particularly if we think about unsupervised or semi-supervised or generative models,

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:47.770
because to some extent a lot of successes that we've seen very recently

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.770
is due to supervised learning.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:55.770
And back in the early days, unsupervised learning was primarily viewed as unsupervised pre-training

00:07:55.770 --> 00:07:59.770
because we didn't know how to train these multilayer systems.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:05.770
And even today, if you're working in a settings where you have lots and lots of unlabeled data

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:09.770
and a small fraction of labeled examples, you know, these unsupervised pre-training models,

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:15.770
so building these generative models can help, you know, for supervised tasks.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:20.770
So I think that a lot of us in the community, you know, it's kind of like it was the belief,

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:23.770
when I started doing my PhD, it was all about generative models

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:26.770
and trying to learn these stacks of models,

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:30.770
because that was the only way for us to train these systems.

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:35.770
Today, there is a lot of work right now on generative modeling.

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:37.770
You know, if you look at generative adversarial networks,

00:08:37.770 --> 00:08:44.770
if you look at variational training course, deep energy models is something that my lab is working on right now as well.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:50.770
I think it's very exciting research, but we haven't, perhaps we haven't quite figured it out.

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:54.770
Again, for many of you who are thinking about getting in the deep learning field,

00:08:54.770 --> 00:09:00.770
this is one area that I think we, you know, will make a lot of progress in, hopefully in the near future.

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:01.770
So unsupervised learning.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:02.770
Unsupervised learning, right.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:03.770
Headlining.

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:06.770
Or maybe you can think of it as unsupervised learning or semi-supervised learning

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:14.770
where you have, I give you some hints or some examples of what different things mean,

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.770
and I throw you lots and lots of unlabeled data.

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:21.770
So, you know, that was actually a very important insight that in an earlier era of deep learning

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:25.770
where computers were just slower, the restricted Boltzmann machine and deep Boltzmann machine,

00:09:25.770 --> 00:09:30.770
that was needed for initializing the neural network weights, but as computers got faster,

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:33.770
straight backprop didn't start to work much better.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.770
So, you know, one other topic that I know you've spent a lot of time thinking about

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:44.770
is the supervised learning versus generative models, unsupervised learning approaches.

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:51.770
So, how has your, tell me a bit about how you're thinking on that debate has evolved over time.

00:09:51.770 --> 00:09:56.770
I think that we all believe that we should be able to make progress there.

00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:03.770
It's just, you know, all the work on Boltzmann machines, variational encoders, GANs,

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:06.770
you can think a lot of these models as generative models,

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:13.770
but we haven't quite figured out how to really make them work

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.770
and how can you make use of large models.

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:24.770
And even for, I see a lot of IT sector, you know, companies have lots and lots of data,

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.770
lots of unlabeled data, there's lots of efforts for going through annotations

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.770
because that's the only way for us to make progress right now.

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:37.770
And it seems like, you know, we should be able to make use of unlabeled data

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:42.770
because it's just abundance of it and we haven't quite figured out how to do that yet.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.770
So, you mentioned for people wanting to enter deep learning research,

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:50.770
you know, unsupervised learning is an exciting area.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:53.770
Today, there are a lot of people wanting to enter deep learning,

00:10:53.770 --> 00:10:55.770
either research or applied work.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:11:00.770
So, for this global community, either research or applied work, what advice would you have?

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:09.770
Yes, I think that one of the key advices I think I should give is people entering that field,

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:15.770
I would encourage them to just try different things and not be afraid to try new things

00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:17.770
and not be afraid to try to innovate.

00:11:17.770 --> 00:11:22.770
I can give you one example, which is when I was a graduate student,

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:26.770
you know, we were looking at neural nets and these are highly non-convex systems

00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:28.770
that are hard to optimize.

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:32.770
And I remember talking to my friends within the optimization community

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:37.770
and the feedback was always that, well, there's no way you can solve these problems

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.770
because these are non-convex, we don't understand optimization,

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:45.770
how could you ever even do that, you know, compared to doing comics optimization?

00:11:45.770 --> 00:11:50.770
And it was surprising because in our lab, you know,

00:11:50.770 --> 00:11:54.770
we never really cared that much about those specific problems,

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:59.770
we're thinking about how can we optimize them, whether we can get interesting results.

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:02.770
And that effectively was driving the community.

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:08.770
So, we were not scared, maybe to some extent because we didn't,

00:12:08.770 --> 00:12:12.770
maybe because we were lacking actually the theory behind optimization.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:18.770
But I would encourage people to just try and not be afraid to try to tackle hard problems.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:22.770
Yeah, and I remember you once said, don't learn to code just into high level,

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:24.770
you know, deep learning frameworks, but actually understand.

00:12:24.770 --> 00:12:28.770
Yes, that's right. I think that, well, it's one of the things that I try to do

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:32.770
when I teach a deep learning class is one of the homeworks,

00:12:32.770 --> 00:12:36.770
I'm asking people to actually code back propagation algorithm

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:38.770
for convolutional neural networks.

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:43.770
And it's, you know, it's painful, but at the same time, if you do it once,

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:48.770
you really understand how these systems operate and how they work

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:52.770
and how you can efficiently implement them on GPU.

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:57.770
And I think it's important for you to, when you go into research or industry,

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:01.770
you have a really good understanding of what these systems are doing.

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.770
So, it's important, I think, you know.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:09.770
Since you have both academic experience as professor and corporate experience,

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:12.770
I'm curious if someone's an entity of learning,

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:17.770
what are their pros and cons of doing a PhD versus joining a company?

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:21.770
Yeah, I think that's actually a very good question.

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:24.770
In my particular lab, I have a mix of students.

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:27.770
Some students want to go and take an academic route.

00:13:27.770 --> 00:13:31.770
Some students want to go and take an industry route.

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:38.770
And it's becoming very challenging because you can do amazing research in industry

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:41.770
and you can also do amazing research in academia.

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:48.770
But in terms of pros and cons, in academia, I feel like you have more freedom

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:51.770
to work on long-term problems.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:55.770
Or if you think about some crazy problem, you can work on it.

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:58.770
So, you have a little bit more freedom.

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:03.770
At the same time, the research that you do in industry is also very exciting

00:14:03.770 --> 00:14:09.770
because in many cases, with your research, you can impact millions of users.

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:16.770
If you develop a core AI technology, and obviously, within the industry,

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.770
you have much more resources in terms of compute

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:24.770
and be able to do really amazing things.

00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:27.770
So, there are pluses and minuses.

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:29.770
It really depends on what you want to do.

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:33.770
And right now, it's an interesting environment

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:38.770
where academics move to industry, and then folks from industry move to academia,

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:39.770
but not as much.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:44.770
And so, it's very exciting times.

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:47.770
It sounds like academic machine learning is great

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.770
and corporate machine learning is great.

00:14:49.770 --> 00:14:53.770
And the most important thing is just jump in. Either one, just jump in.

00:14:53.770 --> 00:14:55.770
So, it really depends on your preferences

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:58.770
because you can do amazing research in either place.

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:03.770
So, you've mentioned unsupervised learning as one exciting frontier for research.

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:07.770
Are there other areas that you consider exciting frontiers for research?

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:08.770
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:11.770
I think that what I see in our community right now,

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:15.770
particularly in the deep learning community, is there are a few trends.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:19.770
One particular area that I think is really exciting

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:22.770
is the area of deep reinforcement learning

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:27.770
because we were able to figure out how we can train ages in virtual worlds

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:31.770
and this is something that's, in just the last couple of years,

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:34.770
you see a lot of progress of how can we scale these systems,

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:36.770
how can we develop new algorithms,

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:40.770
how can we get ages to communicate to each other with each other.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:44.770
And I think that area is, and in general,

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:50.770
the settings where you're interacting with the environment is super exciting.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:55.770
The other area that I think is really exciting as well

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:59.770
is the area of reasoning and natural language understanding.

00:15:59.770 --> 00:16:02.770
So, can we build dialogue-based systems?

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:07.770
Can we build systems that can reason, that can read text

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:11.770
and be able to answer questions intelligently?

00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:16.770
I think this is something that a lot of research is focusing on right now.

00:16:16.770 --> 00:16:20.770
And then there's another sort of sub-area also

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:25.770
is this area of being able to learn from fewer examples.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:28.770
So, typically, people think of it as one-shot learning

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:35.770
or transfer learning, a setting where you learn something about the world

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:40.770
and I throw you a new task at you and you can solve this task very quickly,

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:45.770
much like humans do without requiring lots and lots of labeled examples.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:49.770
And so this is something that a lot of us in the community

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:52.770
are trying to figure out how we can do that

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:57.770
and how can we come closer to human-like learning abilities.

